  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 306 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nSeveral small-scale mid-level impulses, emanating from a \nbroader trough shifting across the southern Rockies, continue \nto eject east-northeast from the Great Plains into the lower \nGreat Lakes this afternoon. The initial impulses have supported \na persistent, steady soaking rainfall across much of the area, \nparticularly along and south of the I-88 corridor. The airmass \nremains unseasonably moist due to a continuous deep northward \nfeed of Gulf moisture. This was confirmed by a derived \nprecipitable water value near 1.7 inches\u2014a near-record high for \nlate September\u2014from a recent Valparaiso University sounding in \nnorthwestern Indiana. Fortunately, a lack of appreciable \ninstability has kept rain rates manageable, generally below \n0.25 inches per hour with only localized corridors reaching 0.5 \ninches per hour. Consequently, no significant hydrological \nconcerns are anticipated for the remainder of the day.\n\nThe focus for this persistent rain is expected to shift \ngradually northward into southern Wisconsin and far northern \nIllinois this evening. Shower coverage should temporarily ease \novernight before the next wave arrives with a cold front on \nThursday. A higher concern for torrential rainfall and \nassociated hydrological impacts exists Thursday afternoon and \nevening as low-level lapse rates steepen ahead of the front. \nPrefrontal instability will remain focused primarily below the \n-10\u00b0C level, which is unfavorable for lightning production but \nhighly conducive to efficient warm-rain processes and low \ncentroid cells given warm-cloud depths reaching 12,000 feet. \n\nWe will have to closely monitor the potential for \nnortheastward- training showers near the frontal boundary \ncapable of rain rates exceeding 1 inch per hour. Where repeated \nheavy rain occurs, localized flash flooding could develop with \ntotals possibly exceeding 3 inches. While footprint amounts over\n3 inches will be localized, broader rainfall totals of 1 to 3 \ninches are expected across the area through Thursday night, \nlikely causing aerial flooding and significant river rises \nthrough the end of the week. These threats remain highlighted in\nour Hydrologic Outlook.\n\nThe surface cold front will push south of the area on Friday,\nbringing an end to the wet pattern as high pressure builds in\nfor the weekend. Dry and cooler conditions\u2014typical for early\nOctober\u2014will prevail for the weekend and early next week, with\nhigh temperatures in the 60s.\n\nKJB",
